  "summary": "I wrote the whole scene in `src/main.ts`. It passes `typecheck`, but I couldn't run it, so I haven't seen a rendered frame. Colours, light levels and the fog/snow tuning are untested. I didn't run `vite build`.\n\n- **Valley:** a procedural flat-shaded terrain mesh forms the V-shaped valley. The slopes are blue-grey and white snow. Steep faces turn dark rock, and instanced angular boulders with snow caps add outcrops. A winding trail climbs the middle, and the valley closes into far peaks.\n- **Trail and cabin:** 23 wooden poles with red tape and snow caps line the trail. A log cabin with a snow-capped roof, chimney and two glowing windows sits on a levelled terrace halfway up. A warm point light lights the snow around it.\n- **Blizzard:**\n  - There are 3000 wind-driven snow flakes, stretched into streaks and angled along the wind, in two sizes (2250 small, 750 large).\n  - They wrap around the camera, so the density stays constant.\n  - A third of the flakes appear only during gusts. Gusts also speed up and lengthen the streaks and raise the fog density.\n- **Sky and fog:** the sky is flat pale grey, with matching exponential fog so far peaks fade to soft silhouettes.\n- **Camera:** it walks the trail at 1.7 m eye height at 1.5 m/s, with bob, lateral sway, a slight roll and a gaze drift. It cuts back to the start after about 95 s. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()`.\n\nThe camera movement depends on `app.camera` being defined. I found the camera feature flag listed as default-on in the engine bundle, and the code calls `app.camera?.setPose` each frame. If the controller is missing at runtime, the camera will stay still at its start pose.\n\n",
